# Git & PR Workflow — CP Build Command Center

This file is shared with the team via the repo. Every contributor (Phil, Hannah, and the dev team) follows these rules. AI agents working in this repo must also follow them.

## Branch Naming

| Who | Pattern | Example |
|-----|---------|---------|
| Phil (backend / full-stack) | `feat/short-description` or `fix/short-description` | `feat/unifier-mock-mode` |
| Hannah (design / UI) | `hannah/feature-name` | `hannah/units-page` |
| RAD devs | `feat/short-description`, `fix/short-description`, `chore/short-description` | `fix/offline-sync-conflict` |

Keep branches focused on one feature or fix. Do not bundle unrelated changes.

## When the Prod Build is Broken (Railway deploy failure)

**Do this before writing a single line of fix code.** Jumping straight to fixes without diagnosing first is how commits end up on the wrong branch.

### Step 1 — Diagnose first

```bash
# What is currently deployed to prod?
git log origin/main --oneline -5

# What is in dev that isn't in prod yet?
git log origin/dev --oneline --not origin/main | head -20

# What files are different between dev and main right now?
git diff origin/main..origin/dev --name-only | head -30

# Does the failing file even exist on dev?
git show origin/dev:path/to/failing/file.ts 2>&1 | head -5
```

Answer these questions before doing anything:
- Which branch does the failing code live on? (`main`? An unmerged feature branch?)
- Is the fix needed in `dev` (because `dev` has the broken file too), or only in `main` (because the file was introduced by the last release)?
- Is the thing Phil wants to deploy already in `main` but blocked by the build error, or is it still sitting in `dev`?

### Step 2 — Create the fix branch from the right base

The fix branch **always** comes from `origin/dev`:

```bash
git checkout -b fix/describe-the-fix origin/dev
```

Never branch from `main` to fix a prod build error. The fix must go through `dev` first.

### Step 3 — Apply the minimum fix

Fix only what is broken. Do not bundle unrelated changes. Do not include files you did not intentionally edit.

### Step 4 — Verify clean working tree before committing

```bash
git status --short   # must show ONLY the files you intentionally changed
git diff --stat      # review every file in the diff
```

If untracked or unrelated files appear, stash or discard them before committing:
```bash
git stash push --include-untracked -- path/to/unrelated/file
```

### Step 5 — Open PR to `dev`, merge, then release to `main`

See The Golden Rule and Production Deployment sections below.

