## Before Every PR — Required Steps

**Always rebase on `dev` before creating a new PR or pushing an update to an existing one.**

```bash
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/dev
# resolve any conflicts, then:
git push --force-with-lease
```

Then run the quality gate:

```bash
npm run build       # must pass — no TypeScript errors
npm run lint        # must pass — 0 errors (warnings OK)
npm run test:unit   # must pass — all unit tests green
```

CI will block the PR if any of these fail, so run them locally first.

## PR Checklist

- [ ] Branch is rebased on `dev` (not behind)
- [ ] `npm run build` passes
- [ ] `npm run lint` passes (0 errors)
- [ ] `npm run test:unit` passes
- [ ] New code has a matching test file (see `.cursor/rules/testing.mdc`)
- [ ] If a new `scripts/bootstrap-*.ts` was added → `scripts/railway-start.sh` is updated in this same PR
- [ ] No secrets, credentials, or `.env` files committed
- [ ] PR targets `dev` (not `main`)
- [ ] `docs/ACTIVE_BRANCHES.md` updated with this branch while it is active
- [ ] **`docs/ACTIVE_BRANCHES.md`:** delete this branch's row when the PR merges to `dev` — same session as merge, or last pre-merge commit on the branch so the merge carries the deletion (never leave merged feature branches listed here)
- [ ] **If `prisma/migrations/` contains a new file**: add the `needs-migration` label and follow the Migration PR Protocol below

## Migration PR Protocol

Any PR that adds a file under `prisma/migrations/` must follow this extra protocol. The goal is to prevent users from ever seeing a live app with unapplied migrations.

### Why this matters

Railway uses zero-downtime deployments. When a new container starts, it runs `prisma migrate deploy` in `railway-start.sh` before `next start`. If the migration fails, the container now exits non-zero and Railway keeps the old container serving. But during the brief startup window before the health check passes, users could hit the old container running code that references tables that don't exist yet. The `/api/health` DB ping gates traffic on DB connectivity, but there is still a window if the migration completes but the tables have unexpected schema.

**The safest guarantee is to verify the migration applied cleanly in dev before ever promoting to prod.**

### Dev verification gate (required before opening the prod PR)

After the PR merges to `dev` and the deploy completes:

```bash
# 1. Confirm the health check is 200 (DB + migration ran)
curl -s https://command-center-reboot-dev.up.railway.app/api/health
# Expected: {"status":"ok",...}  — NOT {"status":"error","reason":"db_unreachable"}

# 2. Confirm the new table(s) actually exist by hitting an API route that uses them
# e.g. for activity_logs:
curl -s "https://command-center-reboot-dev.up.railway.app/api/projects/<any-project-id>/activity?limit=1" \
  -H "Cookie: <your session cookie>" | python3 -m json.tool | head -5
# Expected: JSON with "events" array — NOT {"error":"..."} with status 500
```

Only after both checks pass does Phil approve the prod PR.

### Frontend/backend coordination rule

If a PR introduces **both** a new DB table and UI that calls it, the UI must degrade gracefully if the API returns 500/503. This means:

- The "Failed to load" message the user sees is acceptable as a loading state
- The feature is not listed in release notes until verified in dev
- Do NOT announce the feature to the team until the dev verification gate passes

If the frontend complexity is high (many components touching the new table), consider splitting into:
1. **Backend PR**: migration + API route only, no UI changes
2. **Frontend PR**: UI changes, merged after backend is verified in dev

Splitting is optional for small features but required if the feature has more than 2 API routes backed by new tables.

## PR Labels

| Label | When to use |
|-------|-------------|
| `design` | Hannah's UI/UX work — Phil will review and wire backend |
| `backend` | API routes, database, auth, server-side logic |
| `dependencies` | Dependency bumps (usually Dependabot) |
| `security` | Security fixes or hardening |
| `chore` | Maintenance, docs, config — no feature change |

## Copilot Sub-PR Automation

When `@copilot` addresses review comments it creates a **sub-PR** targeting your branch. When you merge that sub-PR, the automation in `.github/workflows/copilot-sub-pr-automation.yml` will:

1. Auto-resolve all review threads on the parent PR
2. Close any redundant Copilot sub-PRs

**Your steps:** Merge one Copilot sub-PR → automation runs → verify all PR Ready steps pass → notify the user their PR is ready to merge.

See `docs/COPILOT_PR_WORKFLOW.md` for full details.

## What Needs Backend Wiring (Hannah → Phil handoff)

When Hannah's UI needs an API or database change, add this to the PR description or the top of the component:

```tsx
// Backend needed: GET /api/units — list of units with status field
```

Phil will pick this up during review and create the backend work as a follow-up.

