## Copilot Auto-Review Setup — Repository Requirement

Copilot review is triggered by **three** mechanisms, in order of reliability:

### 1. `copilot-review.yml` workflow (PRIMARY — fires automatically on every push that matches its path filter)

`.github/workflows/copilot-review.yml` runs on every `pull_request` event (`opened`, `reopened`, `ready_for_review`, `synchronize`) for PRs targeting `dev` **that touch a path matched by the workflow's `paths:` filter** (security tier and medium-risk tier — see the workflow file for the exact list). Pushes that fall entirely outside those paths (e.g. a pure `docs/**/*.md` follow-up commit after the initial push) will NOT trigger this workflow; use mechanism #2 below in that case.

The reviewer-request step uses:

```bash
gh pr edit "${PR_NUMBER}" --repo "${REPO}" --add-reviewer "@copilot"
```

This command goes through the GraphQL `requestReviews` mutation, which is the **only** API path GitHub currently accepts for adding Copilot as a reviewer (the legacy REST call at `POST .../pulls/<N>/requested_reviewers` with `reviewers[]=copilot` silently no-ops — see DEAD API warning below).

**Why this is primary:** the workflow fires from the GitHub Actions runner without any local agent intervention, and `ubuntu-latest` ships gh CLI ≥ 2.89 which supports `--add-reviewer @copilot` natively (added in gh 2.88, March 2026 changelog). **Authentication:** the `Request Copilot code review` step authenticates with `GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.COPILOT_REVIEWER_PAT }}` — a user-scoped fine-grained PAT with `Pull requests: Read and write` access, stored as a repo secret. The default `GITHUB_TOKEN` silently no-ops for Copilot reviewer requests (verified on PR #677 commits `3f02567` and `63fc6fc`: workflow ran clean, no review ever came; the same command with a user token added Copilot within seconds). The job-level `pull-requests: write` permission is still declared for other step operations (e.g. reading PR data), but the PAT is what makes the reviewer-request mutation succeed. Verified working end-to-end on PR #677 commit `6a698c9`.

### 2. Manual agent-driven re-request (BACKUP — when the workflow's path filter misses)

If a reviewable push didn't match the workflow's `paths:` filter (e.g. a pure `docs/**/*.md` change on a PR that also touched in-scope files earlier), the agent re-requests explicitly:

```bash
gh pr edit <PR_NUMBER> --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --add-reviewer "@copilot"
```

**Requires gh ≥ 2.88.** If the agent's local `gh --version` is older, upgrade it or fall back to the workflow (push a whitespace-equivalent change that matches a filtered path).

**DEAD API — do not use:**

```bash
# ❌ DO NOT USE — silently no-ops since 2026-04-17
gh api --method POST \
  repos/.../pulls/<N>/requested_reviewers \
  -f 'reviewers[]=copilot' \
  # (or 'reviewers[]=copilot-pull-request-reviewer[bot]' — both dead)
```

Returns `200 OK` with a well-formed PR body but does NOT add Copilot or trigger a review. Confirmed via `GH_DEBUG=api` trace and direct `requested_reviewers` readback on PR #677 (see `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` 2026-04-17 entry). A regression unit test (`__tests__/unit/copilot-review-workflow-command.unit.test.ts`) enforces that `copilot-review.yml` does not revert to this pattern.

**When to skip the re-request** — only for pushes that are literally non-reviewable:
- Truly empty commits (`git commit --allow-empty`) with no file changes
- Pure whitespace-only / end-of-line / file-mode diffs
- Lone `package.json` / `package-lock.json` version-bump commits with no other content

Docs and rule changes **are** reviewable and **do** warrant re-request.

#### Verifying the request landed — required after every re-request call

```bash
gh api repos/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/pulls/<PR_NUMBER>/requested_reviewers \
  --jq '[.users[].login]'
# Expected (while the review is pending): ["Copilot"]
# Expected (after Copilot submits the review): [] — the request is cleared
#   from the queue once the review is delivered; check /pulls/N/reviews for
#   the new submission timestamp instead.
```

**This readback is mandatory** — never assume the request succeeded based on exit code alone. If the list is empty AND no recent review exists, the request failed silently; investigate before continuing the loop.

> **Login string inconsistency (not a bug):** the `requested_reviewers`
> endpoint returns `"login": "Copilot"` (the App's display name), while
> the `reviews` endpoint returns `"login": "copilot-pull-request-reviewer"`
> (the bot account login). Both refer to the same entity. Use `["Copilot"]`
> (or `[]` post-review) as the authoritative verification for the re-request
> command; use `copilot-pull-request-reviewer` when parsing review submissions.

### 3. `review_on_push` ruleset (BELT-AND-SUSPENDERS — observed to miss events)

Repository Ruleset ID `13245507` — "Copilot code review"
- Target branches: `~DEFAULT_BRANCH` + `refs/heads/dev`
- Rule: `copilot_code_review` with `review_on_push: true`
- View/edit: https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/rules/13245507

Historically this was treated as the primary mechanism. Empirically it fires sometimes but not reliably — on PR #677 commit `3f02567` (23:54:23) the ruleset never fired despite being correctly configured. Treat it as backup only; the workflow (#1) is authoritative.

**If Copilot stops auto-reviewing NEWLY OPENED PRs:** the ruleset may have reverted to only `~DEFAULT_BRANCH`. Fix:

```bash
gh api --method PUT repos/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/rulesets/13245507 \
  --input - <<'EOF'
{
  "name": "Copilot code review",
  "target": "branch",
  "enforcement": "active",
  "conditions": {
    "ref_name": {
      "include": ["~DEFAULT_BRANCH", "refs/heads/dev"],
      "exclude": []
    }
  },
  "rules": [
    {
      "type": "copilot_code_review",
      "parameters": {
        "review_on_push": true,
        "review_draft_pull_requests": false
      }
    }
  ]
}
EOF
```

