### Agent Communication Protocol — Addressing Copilot Comments

When addressing Copilot review comments, follow this exact communication pattern so Phil always knows what's happening and how far along you are.

#### Step 1 — Announce in chat (before doing anything)

Open with a structured list in the Cursor chat:

```
📋 PR #N — found X Copilot comment(s). Working through them now:

1. `path/to/file.ts` line 42 — <one-line summary of what Copilot flagged>
2. `path/to/other.ts` line 15 — <one-line summary>
...
```

#### Step 2 — Report each fix in chat as you go

After each fix or skip, immediately report it before moving to the next:

```
✅ 1/3 — `path/to/file.ts`: <what you changed and why>
⏸️ 2/3 — `path/to/other.ts`: Skipped — <exact reason>
✅ 3/3 — `path/to/third.ts`: <what you changed and why>
```

#### Step 3 — Post the final ready comment on the PR

Only after ALL comments are addressed (not before):

```bash
gh pr comment <PR_NUMBER> --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
✅ **PR ready for your review, @philipamour**

**Checklist:**
- [x] Branch rebased on latest `dev`
- [x] All CI checks green (lint, unit tests, integration tests, CodeQL)
- [x] Copilot code review complete
- [x] All Copilot comments analyzed (pulled via `gh api graphql`)
- [x] All Copilot comments addressed in code or skipped with documented reasoning
- [x] All conversations resolved on GitHub
- [x] No merge conflicts

**Summary of changes:** <one sentence describing what this PR does>

**Copilot comments actioned:**
- 1/N `path/to/file.ts`: <what Copilot flagged> → Fixed: <what changed> / Skipped: <reason>
- 2/N `path/to/file.ts`: <what Copilot flagged> → Fixed: <what changed> / Skipped: <reason>
EOF
)"
```

#### Step 4 — Close out in chat

```
All done on PR #N. X fixed, Y skipped. Merging now.
```

#### ACTIVE_BRANCHES — drop merged rows immediately

After `state:MERGED` for a feature PR into `dev` (or when you confirm merge from GitHub), **remove that branch's row from `docs/ACTIVE_BRANCHES.md` before the session ends** — same session as the merge receipt, no exceptions. If the row was not removed in a final commit on the feature branch, open or extend the next `dev` PR with that one-line doc fix. Stale rows cause false conflict warnings and wasted coordination.

### After Merging: Verify the Deploy, Then Print the Receipt

Merging to `dev` always triggers the Railway deploy workflow. The `verify` job in that workflow is a hard gate — it polls `/api/health` and fails the entire workflow if HTTP 200 is not returned. **`conclusion=success` on the deploy workflow now means the container is alive. Do not declare success based on anything else.**

#### Phil — hand off to GitHub Actions (watch the full `Deploy` workflow)

Merging to `dev` or `main` triggers a **single** GitHub Actions workflow: **`Deploy`** (`deploy.yml`). The run title in the UI is **`Deploy → <branch> by <actor>`** (e.g. `Deploy → dev by cp-build-dev`). That **one** run orchestrates **Unit Tests → Integration Tests → Build → Deploy to Railway → verify** (plus `generate-tour` / `smoke-test` as configured). The Actions graph and job logs (Vitest summaries, per-job timing, deploy step output) are **the** source of truth for progress—Phil prefers to watch that UI himself rather than read generic agent polling in chat.

- **Do** post **one** in-chat message as soon as the workflow run exists for the triggering commit (see Step 1a). Include:
  - The run title / branch
  - **Direct link:** `https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/actions/runs/<RUN_ID>`
  - A short line that he can follow tests → build → Railway → verify there and hit dev when the run is green.

- **Do not**:
  - Anchor the “tell Phil” milestone on the Railway job alone—the **whole workflow run** is what he opens.
  - Run long **chat-blocking** loops (e.g. sleep 15s × many iterations) narrating `in_progress` / `queued`. That does not match the detail of the GitHub graph and wastes attention.
  - Imply that periodic agent checks are a substitute for the Actions UI.

**When the run finishes:** Still print the full **DEPLOY RECEIPT** (Steps 2–4) once `conclusion` is known—see **completion without chat spinners** below.

#### Step 1a — Find the `Deploy` run for this SHA (brief; then hand off)

Poll **lightly** only until GitHub lists the run (runs can take a few seconds to appear). Cap roughly **~2 minutes** of retries (e.g. 8 × 15s), not a long blocking session.

```bash
BRANCH="dev"  # or "main" for prod
MERGE_SHA=$(gh pr view <PR_NUMBER> --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
  --json mergeCommit --jq '.mergeCommit.oid')

DEPLOY=""
for i in $(seq 1 8); do
  RAW=$(gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
    --workflow deploy.yml --branch "$BRANCH" --commit "$MERGE_SHA" --limit 1 \
    --json databaseId,headSha,status,conclusion,displayTitle)
  DEPLOY=$(echo "$RAW" | jq -c '.[0] // empty')
  [ -n "$DEPLOY" ] && [ "$DEPLOY" != "null" ] && break
  sleep 15
done

if [ -z "$DEPLOY" ] || [ "$DEPLOY" = "null" ]; then
  # Do not run jq on empty JSON — post the branch workflow filter URL instead
  echo "Deploy run not listed yet for merge SHA — open Actions: https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/actions/workflows/deploy.yml?query=branch%3A${BRANCH}"
else
  RUN_ID=$(echo "$DEPLOY" | jq -r '.databaseId')
  RUN_TITLE=$(echo "$DEPLOY" | jq -r '.displayTitle')
  echo "Hand off: $RUN_TITLE — https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/actions/runs/$RUN_ID"
  # Retain MERGE_SHA and RUN_ID for Step 1b / recovery (same deploy run only)
fi
```

If the run never appears after that window, the `if` branch above already gives Phil a direct link to filter `deploy.yml` on the right branch.

#### Step 1b — Completion check (no multi-minute chat poll)

**Do not** block this chat on a loop until `status=completed`. Instead:

- On Phil’s **next** message (any topic): if there is a **pending** deploy for the session’s merge SHA and the receipt was **not** printed, run **one** `gh run list` / `gh run view` for that run. If `completed` → Steps 2–4 (receipt or failure diagnosis). If still `in_progress` → one line: still running, same Actions link—**no** sleep loop in this response.
- If Phil asks deploy status or explicitly says to wait until done, you may poll **in that response only** until complete or a reasonable timeout, then receipt—but default is **hand off + resume on next message**.

#### If the conversation moved on — recover proactively

At the start of a response, if a deploy was triggered earlier and **no receipt** was printed yet, run **one** status check scoped to **the same merge SHA** as Step 1a (never `--branch` + `--limit 1` alone — that can match someone else’s deploy). Use the stored `MERGE_SHA` and `BRANCH`, or `RUN_ID` from the handoff with `gh run view "$RUN_ID" --json status,conclusion`.

```bash
BRANCH="dev"   # same as Step 1a
MERGE_SHA="…"  # same merge commit oid as Step 1a
DEPLOY=$(gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
  --workflow deploy.yml --branch "$BRANCH" --commit "$MERGE_SHA" --limit 1 \
  --json databaseId,headSha,status,conclusion,displayTitle \
  --jq '.[0]')
echo "$DEPLOY" | jq -r '"\(.status)/\(.conclusion // "pending") id:\(.databaseId) \(.displayTitle)"'
```

Never assume the deploy is fine without the receipt path once the run has **succeeded**—always curl health when printing success (Step 3).

**Re-fetch before Steps 2–4:** If time has passed since Step 1a, run the same `gh run list ... --commit "$MERGE_SHA"` again so `DEPLOY` reflects the current `status` / `conclusion`.

#### Step 2 — If `conclusion=failure`: diagnose and fix before notifying Phil

```bash
RUN_ID=$(echo "$DEPLOY" | jq -r '.databaseId')
gh run view "$RUN_ID" --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --log-failed 2>&1 | tail -50
```

Fix the root cause, push to a new branch, open and merge a follow-up PR. Only proceed once the deploy workflow shows `conclusion=success`.

#### Step 3 — If `conclusion=success`: independently confirm health and print the receipt

Even though the `verify` job already confirmed HTTP 200 in CI, independently curl the endpoint and print the full receipt to chat. This is unconditional — no judgment, no skipping.

```bash
# Dev
HEALTH_URL="https://command-center-reboot-dev.up.railway.app/api/health"
# Prod
# HEALTH_URL="https://command-center-reboot-production.up.railway.app/api/health"

HEALTH_RESPONSE=$(curl -s "$HEALTH_URL" 2>/dev/null || echo '{"error":"unreachable"}')
HTTP_CODE=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" "$HEALTH_URL" 2>/dev/null || echo "000")
RUN_ID=$(echo "$DEPLOY" | jq -r '.databaseId')
```

Then print this receipt in chat (include all three checkpoints every time, no exceptions):

```
✅ DEPLOY RECEIPT — [dev|prod] — [$(date +"%l:%M:%S %p" | sed 's/^ //')]

Checkpoint 1 — CI (all jobs must show ✅):
  https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/actions/runs/<RUN_ID>
  unit-tests ✅  integration-tests ✅  build ✅  deploy ✅  verify ✅

Checkpoint 2 — Health endpoint (open in browser to verify independently):
  https://command-center-reboot-[dev|production].up.railway.app/api/health
  Response: <HEALTH_RESPONSE>

Checkpoint 3 — App (open to confirm it loads):
  https://command-center-reboot-[dev|production].up.railway.app/en
```

**If `HTTP_CODE` is not 200 after `conclusion=success`:** this is a contradiction — the `verify` job passed but health is now failing. Treat this as a post-deploy regression. Do NOT print the receipt. Diagnose immediately.

#### Step 4 — Check for workflows awaiting approval

```bash
gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --limit 20 \
  --json databaseId,name,conclusion,headBranch \
  --jq '.[] | select(.conclusion == "action_required") | "⚠️  APPROVAL NEEDED: https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/actions/runs/\(.databaseId) — \(.name) (\(.headBranch))"'
```

Include any approval-needed runs in the receipt message.

#### Step 5 — Wait for Phil to verify, then offer the prod PR

```
Verify in dev when ready — open Checkpoint 3 to confirm.
Say the word and I'll open the prod PR.
```

Do NOT open the prod PR until Phil explicitly approves.

